Three Server Web Infraestructure

The step up

1-Load Balancer (HAProxy)

-This sits at the front door
-Users connect to it first and decides wich backend server should handle the request

2-Server A

-Runs Nginx (web server)
-Runs the application server with the application code
-Has MySQL database (configured as primary)

3-Server B

-Same as Server A: Nginx plus App server plus code
-Has a MySQL replica that copies data from server A


Why add these extra parts?

-Load balancer -> prevents one server from being overwhelmed, spread traffic between two servers and helps if one goes down
-Second server -> allows more users to be served at the same time and reduces dwontime risks
-Database replication -> keeps a backup copy of the data ready to use if the primary fails


Load balancer details

-Primary (on server A) -> all writes (insert, update, delete) go here
-Replica (on server B) -> gets updates from the primary (usually asynchronous). Its great for reads as a backup

From the applications view

-Writes must always go to the primary
-Reads can go to both but the replica may lag slightly behind


Big Picture

-User enters www.foobar.com
-DNS sends them to the load balancer IP
-The load balancer (HAProxy) distributes the request to server A to server B
-Nginx handles it static files are served directly, dynamic ones go through the app server
-The app server may query the database (writes go to primary, reads could come from primary replica)
-Response goes back to the user through the load balancer
